<p:notes xmlns:a="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main" xmlns:p="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/presentationml/2006/main" xmlns:r="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships" xmlns:p14="http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/powerpoint/2010/main" xmlns:p15="http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/powerpoint/2012/main" xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"><p:cSld><p:spTree><p:nvGrpSpPr>        <p:cNvPr id="1" name=""/>        <p:cNvGrpSpPr/>        <p:nvPr/>      </p:nvGrpSpPr>      <p:grpSpPr>        <a:xfrm>          <a:off x="0" y="0"/>          <a:ext cx="0" cy="0"/>          <a:chOff x="0" y="0"/>          <a:chExt cx="0" cy="0"/>        </a:xfrm>      </p:grpSpPr><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="197" name="PlaceHolder 1"></p:cNvPr><p:cNvSpPr><a:spLocks noGrp="1"/></p:cNvSpPr><p:nvPr><p:ph type="sldImg"/></p:nvPr></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="685800" y="1143000"/><a:ext cx="5486040" cy="3085920"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:ln w="0"><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:sp><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="198" name="PlaceHolder 2"></p:cNvPr><p:cNvSpPr><a:spLocks noGrp="1"/></p:cNvSpPr><p:nvPr><p:ph type="body"/></p:nvPr></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="685800" y="4400640"/><a:ext cx="5486040" cy="3600000"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln w="0"><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr><p:txBody><a:bodyPr lIns="91440" tIns="45720" rIns="91440" bIns="45720" anchor="t"><a:noAutofit/></a:bodyPr><a:p><a:pPr indent="0" algn="l"><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2000" b="0" u="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="000000"/></a:solidFill><a:effectLst></a:effectLst><a:uFillTx/><a:latin typeface="Arial"/></a:rPr><a:t>ASP.NET Core&apos;s preview work points toward safer behavior implemented closer to the framework. Asynchronous validation for Minimal APIs allows validation logic to use asynchronous dependencies without forcing blocking patterns. Automatic CSRF protection reduces the chance that a team forgets a security control in an applicable request flow, although developers still need to understand when the protection applies. OpenAPI 3.2 became the default in Preview 6, and Preview 7 added support for representing Server-Sent Events in OpenAPI. This matters for generated clients, documentation, and contract governance. Blazor added a different set of operational features. Circuits can pause after inactivity, server-rendered output can be cached through CacheView, QuickGrid navigation improved, and new analyzers help detect problematic code. These are not isolated conveniences. They move common concerns such as validation, request security, API description, resource use, and diagnostics into framework-supported paths. Teams should review defaults carefully during migration because safer or newer behavior can reveal assumptions in existing applications.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2000" b="0" u="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="000000"/></a:solidFill><a:effectLst></a:effectLst><a:uFillTx/><a:latin typeface="Arial"/></a:endParaRPr></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="0" algn="l"><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2000" b="0" u="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="000000"/></a:solidFill><a:effectLst></a:effectLst><a:uFillTx/><a:latin typeface="Arial"/></a:rPr><a:t>Sources:</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" sz="2000" b="0" u="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="000000"/></a:solidFill><a:effectLst></a:effectLst><a:uFillTx/><a:latin typeface="Arial"/></a:endParaRPr></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="0" algn="l"><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" sz="2000" b="0" u="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="000000"/></a:solidFill><a:effectLst></a:effectLst><a:uFillTx/><a:latin typeface="Arial"/></a:rPr><a:t>https://devblogs.microsoft.com/dotnet/dotnet-11-preview-6/</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" 